--TEST--
A program that keeps every part `Core\Process\Handle::readStdout` returns is stopped by `[limits] memory`, because what a blocking-pool job hands back is charged to the request that takes it — `rule:programs/memory-priority`, `rule:errors/on-limit`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Process\Handle::readStdout
// Each part is read on a pool thread and freed on the core. The balance is per
// thread, so without `nvs_host::blocking::run` moving the charge across, every
// read gave the request a credit of the whole read buffer and this loop ran past
// any ceiling. The loop stops by itself at 64 MB kept, four times the ceiling,
// so a red case fails with a line of output rather than taking the host with it.

string $path = "/usr/bin/yes";
array<string> $arguments = ["a line that repeats and repeats"];
if (Core\Path::SEPARATOR == "\\") {
    $path = "C:\\Windows\\System32\\cmd.exe";
    $arguments = ["/c", "for /l %i in (0,0,1) do @echo a line that repeats and repeats"];
}
Core\Process\Handle $program = Core\Process::spawn($path, $arguments);

// `--EXPECT--` is exact, so this line being the whole of stdout is what asserts
// the loop was stopped while it kept parts.
echo "before\n";
array<bytes> $kept = [];
uint $size = 0;
bool $ended = false;
while (!$ended && $size < 67108864) {
    ?bytes $part = $program->readStdout();
    if ($part != null) {
        $kept[] = $part;
        $size = $size + Core\Bytes::length($part);
    } else {
        $ended = true;
    }
}
echo "kept ", $size, " bytes\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
# The child is named absolutely, and the ceiling is the one the loop above crosses.
[capabilities.process]
exec = true

[limits]
memory = "16M"
--EXPECT--
before
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
FATAL: the request exceeded its memory limit — %d bytes held against a ceiling of 16777216, of which 1048576 is reserved for the limit handler, when an allocation of %d bytes was refused
